<stack-static>
    <statement><p><p><m>P({15}^\circ\,\mathrm{N}, {25}^\circ\,\mathrm{E})</m> and <m>Q({15}^\circ\,\mathrm{N}, {155}^\circ\,\mathrm{W})</m> are on opposite meridians. Kamau says: <q><m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> have the same latitude, so the shortest way from <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> is along their parallel.</q></p><p>(a) Find the distance from <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> along the parallel, to the nearest nautical mile.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans1"/> nm  </p><p>(b) Find the distance from <m>P</m> to <m>Q</m> along the great circle through the North Pole, in nautical miles.</p><p><fillin characters="8" name="ans2"/> nm  </p><p>(c) Which statement is right?</p><p/><ol marker="(1)">
<li>Kamau is right: points on the same parallel are joined most directly along it.</li>
<li>Kamau is wrong: the route over the pole is part of a great circle, and it is shorter.</li>
<li>Kamau is right: the two routes are the same length.</li>
<li>Kamau is wrong: the parallel route is longer only because it crosses the (180^circ) meridian.</li>
</ol>  </p></statement>
    <solution><p><p>(a) The longitudes add to <m>{25}^\circ + {155}^\circ = 180^\circ</m>, so <m>P</m> and <m>Q</m> are half way round their parallel:</p><p><me>180 \times 60 \times \cos {15}^\circ = {10432.0} \approx {10432} \text{ nm}.</me></p><p>(b) The two meridians are opposite, so together they form a great circle through the poles. From <m>P</m> to the North Pole is <m>90^\circ - {15}^\circ = {75}^\circ</m>, and the same again on to <m>Q</m>: <m>{75}^\circ + {75}^\circ = {150}^\circ = {9000}'</m>, so <m>{9000}</m> nm.</p><p>(c) The route over the pole is shorter. A parallel is a small circle, and the shortest path between two points on the surface follows the great circle through them, so Kamau is wrong.</p></p></solution>
    <answer><p><m>10431.9989239</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>9000</m></p></answer>
    <answer><p><m>(2)</m></p></answer>
</stack-static>